LINES=[
("[shouting excitedly]","In 1731, a British captain claimed Spanish sailors cut off his ear! And that ear gave a whole war its name!"),
("[excited]","His name was Robert Jenkins, and his ship, the Rebecca, was stopped near Cuba by a Spanish coast guard, hunting for smugglers."),
("[furious] [shouting]","Jenkins said they tied him up, and the Spanish captain sliced off his ear with a sword! And he said the captain told him: Go tell your king, I'll do the same to him!"),
("[mischievously]","Legend says Jenkins kept the ear, in a jar, and carried it home to England."),
("[building tension] [excited]","In 1738, he was questioned by Parliament. And legend says, he even held up the ear! But historians doubt that!"),
("[serious]","Still, the press and the politicians screamed for revenge. The prime minister, Robert Walpole, did not want a war, but in 1739, Britain declared war on Spain!"),
("[laughing] [excited]","The name came much later, in 1858, from the writer Thomas Carlyle: The War of Jenkins' Ear!"),
("[excited] [dramatic]","In 1741, Admiral Vernon attacked the Spanish city of Cartagena, with about one hundred and eighty ships!"),
("[shouting excitedly]","But Spain had Blas de Lezo, with only six ships, and about three thousand men. A tough admiral, with one eye, one arm, and one leg!"),
("[excited]","And Spain won! Thousands of British soldiers died, most of them from disease."),
("[mischievously] [laughing]","Even funnier, legend says Vernon had victory medals made, before the battle was won!"),
("[dramatic] [excited]","The war merged into an even bigger war, that lasted until 1748. And historians say the ear was just an excuse, the real fight was about trade!"),
("[cheerful] [excited]","So if you like history, where the truth is stranger than the legend, follow for more stories like this!"),
]
